lf the fact that  these different species look so  different from one another and that the  only things really tying them together  were mouthlessness and bacterial  companions meant that scientists had to  ask the question are these different  species actually related it would not be  the first or last time that we'd been  LED astray by a few coincidental  similarities but when they studied the  kentroforosis DNA they found that the  species are in fact related to each  other suggesting that they may have  evolved this relationship with their  bacterial friends long ago and spread  that partnership all over the world  when you look at the bacteria on their  own you can see their rod shape more  clearly these bacteria are primarily  thiotrophs meaning that they live on  sulfur when researchers dug into the  genetics of these bacteria they found  that they were all members of the same  clade gamma proteobacteria a type of  bacteria that includes more famous  sulfur bacteria like begiatoa but  additional work found evidence of  another type of bacteria in that coat as  well called  mirabaculaciai a branch of bacteria that  includes residents of our own guts  now we mentioned earlier that James  found these Kendra for us and samples  that came from the Adriatic coast and  that's no accident kentroforos are  usually found in Marine sediments and  that is thanks in part to the  thyotrophic bacteria that live on their  surface their relationship reminds us a  bit of paramecium bursaria and the green  algae chlorella that live as  endosymbionts inside of vacuoles  specially designed to hold them  chlorella love Sun using it to drive  their photosynthesis so that they can  make their own food and so to suit the  needs of their internal residence  paramecium bursaria are also drawn to  well-lit areas  it is an exchange that works very well  for both the algae and the paramecium  the algae gets the safety of the  paramecium's body and the paramecium  gets some of the nutritional benefit of  whatever the algae has made the Kendra  forest's relationship with their  thyotrophic bacteria is similar except  that the bacteria live on the  kentroforos instead of inside them and  instead of being drawn to light the  cantroforos are drawn instead to that  band of water that hovers above the  sediment where oxygen is low and there's  just enough hydrogen sulfide for the  bacteria to convert into food by letting  themselves be drawn to these areas the  kentroforos benefits as well taking in  some of the metabolites made by the  bacteria and it seems that this exchange  has paid off enough for both the  bacteria and the contraforos the  kentroforo Sicilia is distributed in  such a way to make sure that there are  specific places kept free and clear here  for their surface tenants and the  pockets we described some Kendra foro  species as having maximized the amount  of space available for the bacteria to  attach themselves to Meanwhile the  Kendra forosis bacteria divide in a way  that is not typically seen but that  makes sense when you remember how it  lives typically a rod-shaped bacteria  would divide down the middle along its  shorter axis but these bacteria tear  apart down the longer axis breaking  apart into a sort of v-shape that then  splits in two and why would it do that  because it lets them stay attached to  their host as they divide hooked on  through the root of the V until it's  ready to completely separate into two  now this all seems to be a fairly  balanced well-managed relationship of  the microcosmos the bacteria get access  to sulfur the kentroforos gets access to  nutrients what could possibly go wrong  well a lot if you are the bacteria  because sometimes the cantroforos gets  hungry too hungry  a few metabolites from their bacterial  buddies just aren't enough anymore but  the bacteria themselves well that is a  nice snack to have on hand the  kentroforos may be mouthless but it is  not harmless it has a trick up its  sleeve  phagocytosis it simply absorbs the  bacteria into itself and begins to eat  in some descriptions of the relationship  between the kentroforos and bacteria the  scientists Bland Finley and Tom finchel  have used the phrase kitchen Garden to  describe them which is a quaint image  from the kentroforus's point of view  it's kind of sweet after all to imagine  the ciliate raising a field of bacteria  fertilizing it with trips through anoxic  Waters and tending to its needs  but from the bacteria's point of view it  must feel like a bit of a betrayal like  the whole ground beneath their feet just  opened up and swallowed them whole and  in our imagination that seems like a far  worse place to exist if you were to ask  the simple question who would you rather  be the answer here feels like an easy  one that seems much more appealing to be  the creature who chooses to consume the  other not the one who goes from friend  to phagocytos without warning but maybe  we are being too human about this the  relationship between these bacteria and  the syriad has gone on for a long time  covering oceans on opposite sides of the  planet there is something about this  that must work for the bacteria even if  we do not have the capacity to imagine  it working for us perhaps there is  something to this life attached to the  side of another creature's body  surviving as countless copies of  yourself endure  perhaps there's something to all that  makes sense when it's all that you've  known and perhaps it is our life our way  of imagining ourselves as whole complete  individuals rather than as a single note  in the broader Symphony of our species  that is truly bizarre  thank you for coming on this journey  with us as we explore the Unseen World  that surrounds us and thank you again to  Ren for sponsoring this episode Ren is a  website where you can calculate your  carbon emissions then offset them by  funding projects like ones that help  improve the transparency and scientific  Integrity of carbon removal and climate  Solutions through open data tools and  Leadership we will need a lot of  different approaches to stop the climate  crisis and this is one way that you can  learn more about your carbon  contribution and take some action you'll  answer a few questions about your  lifestyle and they'll also show you ways  you can start reducing your carbon  emissions now no one can reduce their  footprint to zero yet but using Ren you  can offset what you have left once you  sign up you'll receive updates from the  tree planting rainforest protection and  other projects you support and we have  partnered with Ren to plant 10  additional trees for the first 100  people who sign up using the link in our  description  the people on your screen right now are  the folks who make it possible for us to  scour the world for the microbes we want  to share with the world this is a big  beautiful planet and I think part of  what makes a living here worthwhile is  paying a lot of concentrated attention  to it and these people here on the  screen I think they agree and if you'd  like to become one of them you can go to  patreon.com journey to micro where we  have a bunch of cool perks if you want  to see more from our master of  microscopes James Weiss you can check  out jam and germs on Instagram and if  you want to see more from us there's  always a subscribe button somewhere  nearby  [Music]
 

